   "text": {
    "L1": "The 11th and 12th centuries were a time of turning tides: power was shifting in different parts of India.",
    "L2": "In the south, the Cholas became a great power. Rajaraja I completed the Brihadeeswara temple at Thanjavur around 1010 CE.",
    "L3": "Rajendra I carried Chola power to the Ganga, founded Gangaikonda Cholapuram, and sent a navy to Southeast Asia around 1025.",
    "L4": "Chola villages also had assemblies. At Uttaramerur, qualified candidates' names went into a pot, and a child drew the tickets by lot.",
    "L5": "Now bust the myth: Mahmud of Ghazni raided north India for wealth, but he did not conquer and rule most of India.",
    "L6": "Other kingdoms also built and created, including the Chandelas, Paramaras, Solankis and Hoysalas. Their temples show that invasions were not the only story.",
    "L7": "Put these events in time order to check the changing pattern of power."
   },
